# Why Fedora?

## Project Background

When selecting an operating system for this project, I intentionally chose Fedora Linux instead of more commonly used beginner distributions such as Ubuntu.

## Why Fedora?

Part of the goal of this project was to step outside of my comfort zone and gain experience with a Linux distribution that I had not previously worked with extensively.

I wanted the project to be both educational and challenging. Choosing Fedora allowed me to explore a different ecosystem, package management system, and administrative workflow while building a real-world monitoring environment.

## Learning Through Exploration

One of the best ways to learn technology is by working with unfamiliar tools and solving problems along the way.

Rather than selecting the easiest option, I wanted to expose myself to new concepts and gain experience troubleshooting issues in an environment that was new to me. This approach helped strengthen my Linux administration skills and increased my confidence working across different Linux distributions.

## Enterprise Relevance

Fedora is known for adopting newer technologies and features before they are incorporated into enterprise-focused distributions such as Rocky Linux and Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL).

Working with Fedora provided exposure to modern Linux tools and helped build familiarity with technologies that may eventually appear in enterprise environments.

## Project Outcome

Using Fedora made the project more engaging and educational. It encouraged problem-solving, required additional research, and provided valuable hands-on experience beyond simply following installation guides.

The decision reinforced an important lesson: growth often comes from working with technologies that are unfamiliar rather than always choosing what is comfortable.
